part.text:
The fix is complete and validated.

**Change** in `lib/adapters/http.js:363`:

```js
if (config.maxBodyLength > -1) {
  options.maxBodyLength = config.maxBodyLength;
} else {
  options.maxBodyLength = Infinity;
}
```

Previously, when `maxBodyLength` was the default `-1` (unlimited), axios didn't pass `options.maxBodyLength` to `follow-redirects`, which caused it to silently enforce its own 10MB default whenever redirects were enabled (`maxRedirects > 0`), while plain `http`/`https` (used with `maxRedirects: 0`) had no limit. Now `-1` is explicitly mapped to `Infinity`, so the default is consistently unlimited regardless of `maxRedirects`, matching the documented axios default.

**Test results:**
- `should properly support default max body length (follow-redirects as well)` — fails on baseline, passes with the fix
- `should support max body length` — passes
- Full suite: 56 passing, 2 failing — both (`should support max content length`, `should support HTTPS protocol`) also fail on unmodified baseline under load (timing-sensitive/environment issues, unrelated to this change)
